1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,989
Welcome to Unit Six of Algebra One:
systems of equations.

2
00:00:04,439 --> 00:00:09,773
A system is two or more equations that
share the same variables. In this lesson,

3
00:00:09,773 --> 00:00:11,906
we'll solve systems by graphing.

4
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:16,980
A solution of a system is a point that
makes every equation true.

5
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:19,243
On a graph, it's where the lines cross.

6
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:25,273
Solve y equals x plus one, and y equals
negative x plus five.

7
00:00:25,723 --> 00:00:31,294
Graph both lines. They cross at two comma
three. So the solution is x equals two, y

8
00:00:31,294 --> 00:00:32,166
equals three.

9
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,836
Check it in both equations.

10
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:39,681
Two plus one is three. Negative two plus
five is three. Both are true.

11
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:47,018
If the equations are in standard form, use
intercepts. x plus y equals four crosses

12
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:50,613
the axes at four comma zero and zero comma
four.

13
00:00:51,063 --> 00:00:57,016
x minus y equals two crosses at two comma
zero and zero comma negative two. The

14
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:59,277
lines meet at three comma one.

15
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,685
Graphing works well when the answer is
made of whole numbers.

16
00:01:04,135 --> 00:01:09,530
If the lines cross at a messy point, like
one third comma five sevenths, it's hard

17
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:12,754
to read. The next two lessons give exact
methods.

18
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:20,162
Your turn. Solve by graphing: y equals two
x minus one, and y equals negative x plus

19
00:01:20,162 --> 00:01:20,535
five.

20
00:01:20,985 --> 00:01:22,435
Pause the video and try it.

21
00:01:22,885 --> 00:01:28,188
The lines cross at two comma three. Check:
two times two minus one is three, and

22
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:30,309
negative two plus five is three.

23
00:01:31,433 --> 00:01:35,657
Let's solve a system of two lines by
graphing and checking the intersection.

24
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:42,924
Solve this system by graphing: y equals
two x minus three, and x plus y equals

25
00:01:42,924 --> 00:01:43,372
three.

26
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:49,449
The first line is already in slope
intercept form. Start at negative three

27
00:01:49,449 --> 00:01:50,921
and rise two, run one.

28
00:01:52,066 --> 00:01:57,314
Rewrite the second line by subtracting x.
y equals negative x plus three.

29
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:01,057
Start at three, then go down one and right
one.

30
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:03,821
The lines cross at two, one.

31
00:02:04,966 --> 00:02:10,052
Check in both equations. One equals four
minus three, and two plus one equals

32
00:02:10,052 --> 00:02:11,174
three. Both work.

33
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:15,585
The solution is two, one, the point where
the lines intersect.

34
00:02:16,733 --> 00:02:21,281
Watch out. Put both lines in slope
intercept form before graphing. Always

35
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:26,204
check the point in both equations. And
remember graphing only gives an estimate

36
00:02:26,204 --> 00:02:28,509
when the answer isn't a whole number.

37
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:35,702
Your turn. Solve by graphing: y equals x
plus one, and y equals negative two x plus

38
00:02:35,702 --> 00:02:38,188
seven. Pause the video and try it.

39
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:47,837
The lines meet at two, three. Check: two
plus one is three, and negative four plus

40
00:02:47,837 --> 00:02:48,770
seven is three.

41
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:55,383
Substitution solves a system exactly, by
replacing one variable with an expression.

42
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:01,366
Step one: solve one equation for one
variable. Step two: substitute that

43
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:03,732
expression into the other equation.

44
00:03:04,182 --> 00:03:07,830
Step three: solve, then plug back in to
find the other variable.

45
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:12,636
Solve y equals two x, and x plus y equals
nine.

46
00:03:13,086 --> 00:03:19,292
y is already alone, so replace y with two
x in the second equation. x plus two x

47
00:03:19,292 --> 00:03:23,326
equals nine. Three x equals nine, so x
equals three.

48
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:27,731
Plug back in: y equals two times three,
which is six.

49
00:03:28,181 --> 00:03:29,973
The solution is three comma six.

50
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:36,305
Next: x minus two y equals one, and three
x plus y equals seventeen.

51
00:03:36,755 --> 00:03:40,787
Solve the first equation for x: x equals
two y plus one.

52
00:03:41,237 --> 00:03:47,689
Substitute: three times the quantity two y
plus one, plus y, equals seventeen. Seven

53
00:03:47,689 --> 00:03:52,757
y plus three equals seventeen, so y equals
two, and x equals five.

54
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:59,169
Here's a tip. Solve for whichever variable
has a coefficient of one or negative one.

55
00:03:59,619 --> 00:04:01,262
That way, you avoid fractions.

56
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:07,733
Your turn. Solve y equals x minus four,
and two x plus y equals eleven.

57
00:04:08,183 --> 00:04:09,634
Pause the video and try it.

58
00:04:10,084 --> 00:04:16,555
Substitute: two x plus x minus four equals
eleven, so three x equals fifteen and x

59
00:04:16,555 --> 00:04:19,001
equals five. Then y equals one.

60
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:23,845
Here's a worked example of solving a
system by substitution.

61
00:04:25,266 --> 00:04:31,496
Solve by substitution: three x plus two y
equals sixteen, and x minus y equals two.

62
00:04:32,633 --> 00:04:38,137
Pick the easier equation. From x minus y
equals two, x equals y plus two.

63
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:42,466
Substitute y plus two for x in the other
equation.

64
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,837
Distribute the three.

65
00:04:45,966 --> 00:04:52,132
Combine like terms: five y plus six equals
sixteen, so five y is ten and y is two.

66
00:04:53,266 --> 00:04:56,680
Back substitute: x equals two plus two,
which is four.

67
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,293
The solution is four, two.

68
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:05,426
Watch out. Isolate a variable whose
coefficient is one or negative one to

69
00:05:05,426 --> 00:05:10,584
avoid fractions. Use parentheses when you
substitute. And don't stop after one

70
00:05:10,584 --> 00:05:11,906
variable; find both.

71
00:05:13,033 --> 00:05:18,979
Your turn. Solve: y equals three x minus
one, and two x plus y equals nine. Pause

72
00:05:18,979 --> 00:05:20,521
the video and try it.

73
00:05:25,066 --> 00:05:31,179
Substitute: two x plus three x minus one
equals nine, so five x is ten and x is

74
00:05:31,179 --> 00:05:34,816
two. Then y is five. The solution is two,
five.

75
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:41,239
Elimination solves a system by adding the
equations, so that one variable cancels

76
00:05:41,239 --> 00:05:41,501
out.

77
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:46,772
Solve x plus y equals ten, and x minus y
equals two.

78
00:05:47,222 --> 00:05:52,662
Add the equations. The y terms cancel: two
x equals twelve, so x equals six.

79
00:05:53,112 --> 00:05:56,546
Then six plus y equals ten, so y equals
four.

80
00:05:57,666 --> 00:06:03,694
Sometimes you need to multiply first. Take
two x plus three y equals twelve, and x

81
00:06:03,694 --> 00:06:05,090
plus y equals five.

82
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:11,622
Multiply the second equation by negative
two: negative two x minus two y equals

83
00:06:11,622 --> 00:06:12,623
negative ten.

84
00:06:13,766 --> 00:06:17,713
Now add the equations. The x terms cancel,
and y equals two.

85
00:06:18,163 --> 00:06:21,192
Then x plus two equals five, so x equals
three.

86
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:28,194
Sometimes you multiply both equations.
Take three x plus two y equals sixteen,

87
00:06:28,194 --> 00:06:30,824
and two x minus three y equals two.

88
00:06:31,274 --> 00:06:37,274
Multiply the first by three and the second
by two. Now the y terms are six y and

89
00:06:37,274 --> 00:06:38,399
negative six y.

90
00:06:38,849 --> 00:06:44,460
Adding gives thirteen x equals fifty-two,
so x equals four. Then y equals two.

91
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,008
Which method should you use?

92
00:06:47,458 --> 00:06:53,302
Substitution is easiest when a variable is
already alone. Elimination is easiest when

93
00:06:53,302 --> 00:06:55,778
both equations are in standard form.

94
00:06:56,900 --> 00:07:02,340
Your turn. Solve three x plus y equals
eleven, and x minus y equals one.

95
00:07:02,790 --> 00:07:04,240
Pause the video and try it.

96
00:07:04,690 --> 00:07:09,469
Add them: four x equals twelve, so x
equals three. Then y equals two.

97
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,653
Let's solve a system by elimination when
we need to multiply first.

98
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:21,906
Solve by elimination: two x plus three y
equals twelve, and five x minus two y

99
00:07:21,906 --> 00:07:22,948
equals eleven.

100
00:07:24,067 --> 00:07:29,696
To eliminate y, make the coefficients six
and negative six. Multiply the first

101
00:07:29,696 --> 00:07:30,851
equation by two.

102
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,240
Multiply the second equation by three.

103
00:07:35,367 --> 00:07:40,593
Add the equations. The y terms cancel,
leaving nineteen x equals fifty seven.

104
00:07:41,733 --> 00:07:44,080
Divide by nineteen: x equals three.

105
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:50,998
Back substitute into the first original
equation: six plus three y is twelve, so y

106
00:07:50,998 --> 00:07:51,493
is two.

107
00:07:52,633 --> 00:07:54,127
The solution is three, two.

108
00:07:55,267 --> 00:07:59,909
Watch out. When you multiply an equation,
multiply every term, including the

109
00:07:59,909 --> 00:08:04,979
constant. If coefficients are opposites,
add; if they're equal, subtract. And check

110
00:08:04,979 --> 00:08:08,216
in the equation you didn't use for back
substitution.

111
00:08:09,333 --> 00:08:14,683
Your turn. Solve: three x plus four y
equals ten, and two x minus y equals

112
00:08:14,683 --> 00:08:17,141
three. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:08:21,667 --> 00:08:28,340
Multiply the second by four: eight x minus
four y equals twelve. Add: eleven x equals

114
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:31,245
twenty two, so x is two and y is one.

115
00:08:32,366 --> 00:08:38,052
Most systems have exactly one solution. In
this lesson, we'll meet the special cases:

116
00:08:38,052 --> 00:08:40,259
no solution, and infinitely many.

117
00:08:41,399 --> 00:08:44,727
When the lines have different slopes, they
cross exactly once.

118
00:08:45,177 --> 00:08:46,415
That's one solution.

119
00:08:47,533 --> 00:08:52,136
When the lines are parallel, with the same
slope but different y intercepts, they

120
00:08:52,136 --> 00:08:53,954
never meet. There's no solution.

121
00:08:54,404 --> 00:09:00,282
Algebraically, you get a false statement.
Substitute y equals two x plus one into y

122
00:09:00,282 --> 00:09:04,815
equals two x minus three, and you get one
equals negative three.

123
00:09:05,933 --> 00:09:10,654
When both equations describe the same
line, every point on it is a solution.

124
00:09:10,654 --> 00:09:12,269
There are infinitely many.

125
00:09:12,719 --> 00:09:18,503
Algebraically, you get a true statement.
For x plus y equals three and two x plus

126
00:09:18,503 --> 00:09:24,287
two y equals six, multiply the first by
negative two and add. You get zero equals

127
00:09:24,287 --> 00:09:24,644
zero.

128
00:09:25,766 --> 00:09:31,155
To classify a system without solving,
write both equations in slope-intercept

129
00:09:31,155 --> 00:09:31,505
form.

130
00:09:31,955 --> 00:09:36,880
Different slopes: one solution. Same
slope, different intercepts: no solution.

131
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:39,848
Same slope and same intercept: infinitely
many.

132
00:09:40,966 --> 00:09:46,918
Classify y equals negative three x plus
four, and six x plus two y equals eight.

133
00:09:47,368 --> 00:09:52,873
Solve the second for y: y equals negative
three x plus four. It's the same line, so

134
00:09:52,873 --> 00:09:55,261
there are infinitely many solutions.

135
00:09:56,399 --> 00:10:02,215
Your turn. How many solutions does this
system have? y equals one half x plus two,

136
00:10:02,215 --> 00:10:04,271
and x minus two y equals six.

137
00:10:04,721 --> 00:10:06,172
Pause the video and try it.

138
00:10:06,622 --> 00:10:12,049
The second equation becomes y equals one
half x minus three. Same slope, different

139
00:10:12,049 --> 00:10:13,366
intercepts, so there's no solution.

140
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:20,449
Let's identify special systems, ones with
no solution or infinitely many.

141
00:10:21,867 --> 00:10:28,125
How many solutions does each system have?
System a: six x minus three y equals nine,

142
00:10:28,125 --> 00:10:33,936
and y equals two x minus three. System b:
four x plus two y equals five, and y

143
00:10:33,936 --> 00:10:36,245
equals negative two x plus one.

144
00:10:37,367 --> 00:10:40,887
For system a, substitute two x minus three
for y.

145
00:10:42,033 --> 00:10:46,428
The x terms cancel and we get nine equals
nine, which is always true.

146
00:10:47,567 --> 00:10:52,452
Always true means the equations are the
same line. Infinitely many solutions.

147
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:56,992
For system b, substitute negative two x
plus one.

148
00:10:58,133 --> 00:11:02,613
The x terms cancel again, but now two
equals five, which is never true.

149
00:11:03,733 --> 00:11:06,933
Never true means the lines are parallel.
No solution.

150
00:11:08,067 --> 00:11:12,717
System a has infinitely many solutions,
and system b has no solution.

151
00:11:13,867 --> 00:11:18,590
Watch out. If the variables disappear and
you get a true statement, there are

152
00:11:18,590 --> 00:11:22,762
infinitely many solutions. A false
statement means no solution. As a

153
00:11:22,762 --> 00:11:25,216
shortcut, compare slopes and intercepts.

154
00:11:26,333 --> 00:11:32,275
Your turn. How many solutions: y equals
three x plus two, and nine x minus three y

155
00:11:32,275 --> 00:11:35,101
equals six? Pause the video and try it.

156
00:11:39,633 --> 00:11:45,133
The second becomes y equals three x minus
two. Same slope, different intercepts, so

157
00:11:45,133 --> 00:11:46,524
there is no solution.

158
00:11:47,666 --> 00:11:53,074
Systems are perfect for word problems with
two unknowns. In this lesson, we'll set up

159
00:11:53,074 --> 00:11:54,855
and solve two classic types.

160
00:11:55,999 --> 00:12:01,301
Use the same plan as before: define two
variables, write two equations, solve, and

161
00:12:01,301 --> 00:12:02,335
answer in words.

162
00:12:03,466 --> 00:12:08,802
A theater sold one hundred tickets. Adult
tickets cost eight dollars, child tickets

163
00:12:08,802 --> 00:12:12,917
cost five dollars, and sales totaled six
hundred eighty dollars.

164
00:12:13,367 --> 00:12:17,228
Let a be the number of adult tickets, and
c the number of child tickets.

165
00:12:17,678 --> 00:12:22,444
Then a plus c equals one hundred, and
eight a plus five c equals six hundred

166
00:12:22,444 --> 00:12:22,883
eighty.

167
00:12:24,033 --> 00:12:28,406
Substitute a equals one hundred minus c
into the second equation.

168
00:12:28,856 --> 00:12:34,237
Eight hundred minus eight c plus five c
equals six hundred eighty, so negative

169
00:12:34,237 --> 00:12:38,584
three c equals negative one hundred
twenty, and c equals forty.

170
00:12:39,034 --> 00:12:43,879
Then a equals sixty. So the theater sold
sixty adult tickets and forty child

171
00:12:43,879 --> 00:12:44,389
tickets.

172
00:12:45,533 --> 00:12:49,330
Another classic: two numbers add to
thirty, and their difference is eight.

173
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:55,373
x plus y equals thirty, and x minus y
equals eight. Add them: two x equals

174
00:12:55,373 --> 00:12:59,529
thirty-eight, so x equals nineteen and y
equals eleven.

175
00:12:59,666 --> 00:13:02,885
Always check your answer against the
story.

176
00:13:03,335 --> 00:13:08,554
Sixty plus forty is one hundred tickets,
and sixty times eight plus forty times

177
00:13:08,554 --> 00:13:11,527
five is six hundred eighty dollars. It
works.

178
00:13:11,666 --> 00:13:17,819
Your turn. A farm has chickens and cows,
with twenty heads and fifty-six legs in

179
00:13:17,819 --> 00:13:19,365
total. How many of each?

180
00:13:19,815 --> 00:13:21,265
Pause the video and try it.

181
00:13:21,715 --> 00:13:27,549
Chickens plus cows equals twenty, and two
times chickens plus four times cows equals

182
00:13:27,549 --> 00:13:31,230
fifty-six. That gives twelve chickens and
eight cows.

183
00:13:32,366 --> 00:13:36,611
Here's a worked example of a classic word
problem solved with a system.

184
00:13:38,033 --> 00:13:43,292
A café sells coffee for three dollars and
tea for two. One morning it sold seventy

185
00:13:43,292 --> 00:13:47,846
drinks and made one hundred eighty
dollars. How many of each were sold?

186
00:13:48,966 --> 00:13:53,147
Define variables. c is the number of
coffees and t the number of teas.

187
00:13:54,266 --> 00:13:57,103
The count equation: c plus t equals
seventy.

188
00:13:58,233 --> 00:14:01,881
The money equation: three c plus two t
equals one eighty.

189
00:14:02,999 --> 00:14:05,687
Multiply the count equation by negative
two.

190
00:14:06,833 --> 00:14:09,627
Add it to the money equation: c equals
forty.

191
00:14:10,766 --> 00:14:13,667
Then t is seventy minus forty, which is
thirty.

192
00:14:14,799 --> 00:14:17,402
The café sold forty coffees and thirty
teas.

193
00:14:18,533 --> 00:14:23,598
Watch out. Mixture problems usually need
one equation for the count and one for the

194
00:14:23,598 --> 00:14:28,175
value. Say what each variable means. And
answer the question in a sentence.

195
00:14:29,299 --> 00:14:33,702
Your turn. A test has twenty five
questions worth either three or five

196
00:14:33,702 --> 00:14:38,985
points, for ninety five points in all. How
many of each? Pause the video and try it.

197
00:14:43,533 --> 00:14:49,331
x plus y equals twenty five, and three x
plus five y equals ninety five. That gives

198
00:14:49,331 --> 00:14:53,453
fifteen three point questions and ten five
point questions.

199
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:59,529
In this last lesson of Unit Six, we'll
graph systems of inequalities, where the

200
00:14:59,529 --> 00:15:01,213
solution is a whole region.

201
00:15:02,333 --> 00:15:06,067
First, graph one inequality: y is greater
than x plus one.

202
00:15:06,517 --> 00:15:11,558
Draw the line y equals x plus one, dashed,
because points on the line aren't

203
00:15:11,558 --> 00:15:15,007
included. Then shade above it, because y
is greater.

204
00:15:16,133 --> 00:15:21,751
For less than or equal to, or greater than
or equal to, use a solid line, because the

205
00:15:21,751 --> 00:15:22,875
line is included.

206
00:15:23,325 --> 00:15:28,679
y is less than or equal to negative x plus
three: solid line, shade below.

207
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:33,000
The solution of the system is where the
shadings overlap.

208
00:15:33,450 --> 00:15:36,565
Every point in this region works in both
inequalities.

209
00:15:37,700 --> 00:15:42,526
To check which side to shade, test a point
that isn't on the line, like zero comma

210
00:15:42,526 --> 00:15:42,820
zero.

211
00:15:43,270 --> 00:15:48,603
Is zero greater than zero plus one? No. So
shade the side without the origin.

212
00:15:49,733 --> 00:15:51,973
Systems of inequalities model limits.

213
00:15:52,423 --> 00:15:56,989
If you can spend at most twenty dollars,
and you need at least five items, the

214
00:15:56,989 --> 00:15:59,506
overlap shows every combination that
works.

215
00:16:00,633 --> 00:16:06,468
Your turn. Is the point one comma three a
solution of y greater than x plus one, and

216
00:16:06,468 --> 00:16:09,593
y less than or equal to negative x plus
five?

217
00:16:10,043 --> 00:16:11,494
Pause the video and try it.

218
00:16:11,944 --> 00:16:17,141
Three is greater than two: yes. Three is
at most four: yes. So one comma three is a

219
00:16:17,141 --> 00:16:17,704
solution.

220
00:16:18,833 --> 00:16:23,292
Let's graph a system of inequalities and
test points in the solution region.

221
00:16:24,733 --> 00:16:30,201
Graph the system y is at least x minus
two, and y is less than negative one half

222
00:16:30,201 --> 00:16:35,122
x plus three. Then decide whether one, one
and four, zero are solutions.

223
00:16:36,266 --> 00:16:41,453
The first boundary is y equals x minus
two. It's greater than or equal, so the

224
00:16:41,453 --> 00:16:43,648
line is solid and we shade above.

225
00:16:44,766 --> 00:16:50,341
The second boundary is y equals negative
one half x plus three. Strictly less than,

226
00:16:50,341 --> 00:16:52,020
so dashed, shading below.

227
00:16:53,166 --> 00:16:58,331
Test one, one. One is at least negative
one, and one is less than two point five.

228
00:16:58,331 --> 00:16:58,969
Both true.

229
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:02,852
Test four, zero. Zero is at least two?
False.

230
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:09,423
One, one is in the overlapping region, so
it's a solution. Four, zero fails the

231
00:17:09,423 --> 00:17:11,552
first inequality, so it is not.

232
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:17,847
Watch out. Less than or equal and greater
than or equal get solid lines; strict

233
00:17:17,847 --> 00:17:22,344
inequalities get dashed lines. The
solution is only where the shading

234
00:17:22,344 --> 00:17:25,862
overlaps. And a point has to satisfy every
inequality.

235
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,344
Your turn. Is two, three a solution of y
greater than two x minus two, and y at

236
00:17:32,344 --> 00:17:34,914
most four? Pause the video and try it.

237
00:17:39,433 --> 00:17:43,913
Three is greater than two, and three is at
most four. Both are true, so yes.

238
00:17:45,033 --> 00:17:49,623
Let's finish Unit Six with a bake sale
that uses equations and inequalities

239
00:17:49,623 --> 00:17:50,174
together.

240
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:56,117
At a bake sale, cookies sell for one
dollar and brownies for two. Each cookie

241
00:17:56,117 --> 00:18:00,752
costs twenty five cents to make and each
brownie seventy five cents. The supply

242
00:18:00,752 --> 00:18:05,269
budget is thirty dollars. The goal is to
sell exactly sixty items for exactly

243
00:18:05,269 --> 00:18:08,965
eighty five dollars. How many of each, and
is it within budget?

244
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,254
The count: c plus b equals sixty.

245
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,514
The sales: one c plus two b equals eighty
five.

246
00:18:17,633 --> 00:18:21,793
Subtract the first equation from the
second. b equals twenty five.

247
00:18:22,933 --> 00:18:26,389
Then c is sixty minus twenty five, which
is thirty five.

248
00:18:27,533 --> 00:18:32,863
Now the cost: twenty five cents times
thirty five, plus seventy five cents times

249
00:18:32,863 --> 00:18:37,261
twenty five. That's eight seventy five
plus eighteen seventy five.

250
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:42,730
That's twenty seven fifty, which is at
most thirty dollars. Within budget.

251
00:18:43,866 --> 00:18:48,530
They should make thirty five cookies and
twenty five brownies. It costs twenty

252
00:18:48,530 --> 00:18:53,253
seven fifty, under the thirty dollar
budget, for a profit of fifty seven fifty.

253
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:59,936
Watch out. Use equations for exact goals
and inequalities for limits like a budget.

254
00:18:59,936 --> 00:19:05,271
Solve the system first, then test the
constraint. And remember profit is revenue

255
00:19:05,271 --> 00:19:06,005
minus cost.

256
00:19:07,133 --> 00:19:11,872
Your turn. Same prices, but sell fifty
items for eighty dollars. How many of

257
00:19:11,872 --> 00:19:15,240
each, and what's the cost? Pause the video
and try it.

258
00:19:19,766 --> 00:19:25,529
Subtracting gives b equals thirty, so c is
twenty. The cost is five plus twenty two

259
00:19:25,529 --> 00:19:27,958
fifty, which is twenty seven fifty.

260
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:34,307
That completes this unit. Next up: Algebra
I, Unit 7: Exponents and Exponential

261
